Osmosis is the passive transport of water molecules through a selectively permeable membrane from an area of higher water concentration to an area of lower water concentration, always from regions with higher water potential to regions with lower water potential. This process occurs without the expenditure of energy by the cell or any other active mechanism, and it allows for the equalization of water potential on either side of the membrane.

To illustrate this concept, consider two solutions separated by a semipermeable membrane (like the cell membrane in living organisms). If one solution has a higher concentration of solute molecules than the other, the less concentrated solution will move across the membrane to try and balance it. This movement continues until both sides reach the same concentration of particles.

Osmosis is fundamental in biology as water moves through cells and within plant tissues, affecting processes like turgor pressure (in plants) and cell volume regulation.